netstack: keep nftables' connection tracking apart from iptables'

What: nftables keeps a packet's connection tracking and NAT state of its own,
apart from the packet's state for iptables: it swaps its own in for each hook
it evaluates, and back out after. A packet that iptables tracks and NATs, as it
does every packet once any of its tables is set, is then tracked and NATed by
nftables as well, each by its own rules.

Why: under --TESTONLY-nftables both run on every packet, iptables first, and
they shared the packet's conntrack tuple and its NAT-done flags. Once a table
of iptables was set, iptables gave each new connection a NAT, a no-op one when
no rule matched, and nftables' NAT of the same packet then panicked the Sentry
("packet already had NAT: DNAT performed"); at postrouting iptables finalized
nftables' connection, so nftables found none. Orbit sets iptables chains in a
sandbox whose network is sealed or that has a Gateway, and a container engine
in it sets its rules in nf_tables, so the engine's first connection ended the
sandbox.

Verified:
- gVisor's unit tests in its builder container: the new TestSwapNFTablesState
  (pkg/tcpip/stack) and the rest of //pkg/tcpip/stack:stack_test and
  //pkg/tcpip/nftables:nftables_test pass.
- In an Ubuntu 24.04 container on runsc with --TESTONLY-nftables --net-raw,
  with one DNAT rule set by iptables-legacy and one by iptables-nft, each to a
  local server: without this patch the sandbox died at the first connection
  (exit status 2, the panic above); with it a connection to each rule's
  address reached the server its rule names.
- Orbit's conformance and integration suites: a sealed sandbox with Orbit's chains
  in iptables ran Docker 29.1.3, whose rules are in nf_tables; its containers
  reached the Internet while it was open, and the sandbox reached its Gateway.

